WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.572 --> 00:00:20.322
Hello, and welcome to another episode of Superstars in History, the only talk show that lets you meet

2
00:00:20.402 --> 00:00:22.843
some of the most famous people in history!

3
00:00:23.724 --> 00:00:29.808
I'm your host, Faye Moss, and I've got a globetrotting guest for you today.

4
00:00:30.728 --> 00:00:36.931
He was a merchant and explorer who wrote a hit book about his marvelous travels.

5
00:00:37.752 --> 00:00:59.964
Please welcome Marco It's a pleasure to be here.

6
00:01:00.416 --> 00:01:02.737
This may be my longest journey ever.

7
00:01:04.038 --> 00:01:06.759
Well, you're no stranger to long trips.

8
00:01:07.699 --> 00:01:12.241
In fact, traveling for extended periods runs in your family!

9
00:01:13.141 --> 00:01:19.624
When you were born in Venice, Italy, around 1254, your father and uncle were away on a long

10
00:01:19.724 --> 00:01:25.347
trip. Yes, my father and uncle had left on their journey before I was born.

11
00:01:26.467 --> 00:01:32.945
They were wealthy merchants, you see, and in those days merchants traveled far and wide to find beautiful

12
00:01:33.005 --> 00:01:34.426
goods to sell in Europe.

13
00:01:35.526 --> 00:01:41.427
While my father was away, my mother died, so I was raised by relatives.

14
00:01:42.668 --> 00:01:48.489
Years passed, and I was 15 when I met my father for the first time, upon his return

15
00:01:48.549 --> 00:01:49.330
with my uncle.

16
00:01:50.410 --> 00:01:54.791
Fifteen? That was a very, very long trip!

17
00:01:55.611 --> 00:01:57.812
Did they bring you any souvenirs?

18
00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:03.601
They did have a souvenir from the Mongol emperor, Kublai Khan!

19
00:02:04.522 --> 00:02:06.802
But it wasn't for me—it was for the pope.

20
00:02:07.883 --> 00:02:14.184
My father and uncle's journey had taken them all the way to Yuan Dynasty China, where they visited

21
00:02:14.264 --> 00:02:15.085
Kublai Khan.

22
00:02:16.185 --> 00:02:21.206
The khan was thrilled to meet travelers from Italy since he was curious about the pope and the

23
00:02:21.266 --> 00:02:22.147
Catholic Church.

24
00:02:23.087 --> 00:02:28.969
The khan decided to make my father and uncle his ambassadors, and he sent them to Rome with

25
00:02:29.029 --> 00:02:30.791
a personal letter for the pope.

26
00:02:32.092 --> 00:02:36.415
The pope must've been pleased to hear from such an important ruler.

27
00:02:37.475 --> 00:02:39.897
Actually there was no pope at that time!

28
00:02:40.738 --> 00:02:44.340
By the time my father and uncle returned, the pope had died.

29
00:02:45.361 --> 00:02:50.765
The khan was expecting a reply, but it took nearly three years before a new pope was elected.

30
00:02:51.766 --> 00:02:56.933
Finally, with a letter from the new pope to the khan, I set off for China with my

31
00:02:57.033 --> 00:02:59.555
father and uncle on the Silk Road.

32
00:03:00.495 --> 00:03:04.397
That was the overland trading route between Europe and Asia.

33
00:03:05.598 --> 00:03:07.499
I bet it wasn't an easy trip.

34
00:03:08.299 --> 00:03:09.180
What was it like?

35
00:03:10.280 --> 00:03:16.144
By the thirteenth century, the Silk Road had become very dangerous due to wars and bandits.

36
00:03:17.064 --> 00:03:19.846
We encountered blizzards and flooded rivers.

37
00:03:20.826 --> 00:03:25.656
In the Gobi Desert there was nothing to be found to eat, and it took a month to

38
00:03:25.776 --> 00:03:27.797
cross it at its narrowest point.

39
00:03:28.937 --> 00:03:32.178
At its widest, it was said to take a year to cross.

40
00:03:33.378 --> 00:03:41.381
Finally, about three years later, we arrived at Kublai Khan's summer capital, where I met the great khan!

41
00:03:42.481 --> 00:03:49.263
He was delighted that my father and uncle had returned safely, and he welcomed me with enthusiasm.

42
00:03:50.103 --> 00:03:52.444
Tell us more about Kublai Khan!

43
00:03:52.724 --> 00:03:53.684
What was he like?

44
00:03:54.812 --> 00:04:01.074
Not only did he rule a vast empire, which encompassed a large part of Asia, but he was

45
00:04:01.254 --> 00:04:05.216
also very interested in the world beyond his empire.

46
00:04:06.256 --> 00:04:10.498
He welcomed foreigners and the new ideas and knowledge they brought.

47
00:04:11.018 --> 00:04:16.900
He was also tolerant of different religions such as Islam, Buddhism, and Christianity.

48
00:04:17.864 --> 00:04:26.171
It may surprise you, but the Mongol Empire was quite cosmopolitan, with people of different cultures, languages, and

49
00:04:26.251 --> 00:04:27.952
religions living within it.

50
00:04:29.233 --> 00:04:34.597
And, if I may say so myself, I impressed the khan with my intelligence.

51
00:04:35.678 --> 00:04:42.303
He made me his envoy, and I traveled around the empire and beyond, seeing the marvels of the

52
00:04:42.383 --> 00:04:51.222
world. Few Europeans had ever traveled to Asia before, so I imagine you saw things that were very

53
00:04:51.322 --> 00:04:52.443
different from Europe.

54
00:04:53.704 --> 00:04:54.905
Indeed, Faye!

55
00:04:55.825 --> 00:05:00.489
In China the cities were well organized with wide, clean streets.

56
00:05:01.490 --> 00:05:08.796
Houses were heated with coal; people bathed every day; and wealthier citizens even had bathrooms in their homes.

57
00:05:09.816 --> 00:05:13.880
Oh, and they had paper money and beautiful porcelain dishes.

58
00:05:14.972 --> 00:05:17.013
It was very unlike Europe!

59
00:05:18.354 --> 00:05:26.699
You also traveled to Southeast Asia, where you saw many animals—like rhinoceroses, which you thought were unicorns!

60
00:05:28.980 --> 00:05:32.262
Oh yes, those unicorns weren't at all what I expected.

61
00:05:33.083 --> 00:05:38.906
They had thick hair like buffalos, feet like elephants, and heads like boars!

62
00:05:40.067 --> 00:05:44.782
I want to know more about what you saw, but I don't think we have enough time.

63
00:05:45.623 --> 00:05:48.565
Your travels lasted 24 years!

64
00:05:49.726 --> 00:05:51.407
I almost didn't make it home!

65
00:05:52.508 --> 00:05:58.773
Kublai Khan didn't want my father, uncle, and me to leave, and he refused to release me from

66
00:05:58.833 --> 00:05:59.514
his service.

67
00:06:00.575 --> 00:06:06.940
Eventually he agreed, but only because he needed us to escort a Mongol princess to Persia.

68
00:06:07.921 --> 00:06:09.802
We left around 1291, traveling by sea.

69
00:06:12.404 --> 00:06:18.029
It was a long and dangerous journey, but we arrived safely in Venice four years later.

70
00:06:19.030 --> 00:06:23.654
By then we'd been gone so long, that we struggled to speak our native language!

71
00:06:25.515 --> 00:06:28.918
You came home to war and prison.

72
00:06:30.039 --> 00:06:35.924
Yes, Venice was at war with Genoa, so I served my city by buying and commanding a ship.

73
00:06:36.965 --> 00:06:39.768
Sadly I was captured and thrown in prison.

74
00:06:40.492 --> 00:06:42.733
Prison wasn't too bad, but it was boring.

75
00:06:43.794 --> 00:06:47.236
I livened things up by telling stories of my adventures.

76
00:06:48.056 --> 00:06:54.700
A fellow prisoner, Rustichello da Pisa, was a famous story writer who wrote down my tales for a

77
00:06:54.760 --> 00:07:00.062
book— but he may have embellished some details to create excitement for readers.

78
00:07:00.943 --> 00:07:05.485
We published the book after we were released from prison around 1299.

79
00:07:06.406 --> 00:07:10.148
It became popular and was even translated into several languages.

80
00:07:11.168 --> 00:07:18.455
That's impressive, Marco—especially since back then every copy of a book had to be written by hand!

81
00:07:19.275 --> 00:07:25.261
But even though your book was widely read, some people were skeptical about the truth of your stories.

82
00:07:26.262 --> 00:07:30.525
Yes. Some readers claimed I made things up, but they were wrong!

83
00:07:31.546 --> 00:07:33.348
I really had those adventures!

84
00:07:34.389 --> 00:07:37.472
I do admit, however, that I am not a modest man.

85
00:07:38.588 --> 00:07:43.833
I probably overstated a few small details about my influence at the khan's court.

86
00:07:44.594 --> 00:07:50.139
But the records of my travels are quite accurate, and I told only half of what I saw.

87
00:07:51.220 --> 00:07:57.105
Well, your book is certainly well loved—it's stayed in print for over seven hundred years.

88
00:07:57.946 --> 00:08:03.852
Thank you for setting the record straight and for taking another journey this time through history!

89
00:08:04.984 --> 00:08:08.307
I enjoyed my visit to your colorful land very much!

90
00:08:12.309 --> 00:08:18.153
Marco Polo spent the rest of his life raising a family and being a successful merchant in Venice.

91
00:08:19.174 --> 00:08:23.077
He died in 1324 at the age of 69.

92
00:08:24.018 --> 00:08:32.684
Polo's book introduced many Europeans to the cultures and customs of Asia, and inspired and educated other explorers.

93
00:08:33.640 --> 00:08:35.222
Thank you for joining us today.

94
00:08:36.023 --> 00:08:41.308
I'll see you next time when we talk with another superstar in history.
